      "problem": "Rationalize the denominator of $\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{2x}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we apply the quotient rule and put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{2x}} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{2x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to remove the radical $\\sqrt{2x}$ from the denominator. To do this, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{2x},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{2x}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2x}}{\\sqrt{2x}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10x}}{(\\sqrt{2x})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10x}}{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
